========================================================================================================================
                              CREATING AN iSCSI STORAGE POOL & ATTACHING LUNs TO A VM
========================================================================================================================

PHASE 1: CREATING AND ACTIVATING THE EXISTING iSCSI STORAGE POOL
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
When you link libvirt to an existing iSCSI Target, libvirt logs into the portal and scans for available LUNs (Logical 
Unit Numbers). Note that libvirt cannot create or delete volumes on a remote iSCSI target; it only maps existing LUNs.

Step 1   | Define the persistent iSCSI storage pool layout:
         | virsh pool-define-as --name iscsi-pool \
         |                      --type iscsi \
         |                      --source-host 192.168.100.20 \
         |                      --source-path ://2026-08.com.example:storage.target1 \
         |                      --target /dev/disk/by-path
         |
Fields   | --name        : The local unique identifier for your pool (iscsi-pool).
         | --type        : The storage driver architecture (iscsi).
         | --source-host : The IP address or FQDN of your existing iSCSI Target / SAN.
         | --source-path : The target's IQN (iSCSI Qualified Name).
         | --target      : The local directory on the KVM host where iSCSI block device links are exposed.
         |
Step 2   | Configure the pool to automatically log into the iSCSI portal at host system boot:
         | virsh pool-autostart iscsi-pool
         |
Step 3   | Start the storage pool (this triggers the iSCSI initiator login sequence on the host):
         | virsh pool-start iscsi-pool

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 2: IDENTIFYING THE STORAGE VOLUME (LUN)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1   | List all available iSCSI targets discovered inside the activated pool:
         | virsh vol-list iscsi-pool
         |
         | Expected output snippet:
         | Name               Path
         | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         | unit:0:0:1         /dev/disk/by-path/ip-192.168.100.20:3260-iscsi-://2026-08.com.example...lun-1
         |
Step 2   | Copy the exact absolute path returned under the "Path" field for the LUN you wish to attach.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 3: ATTACHING THE iSCSI LUN DIRECTLY TO A SPECIFIC VM
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Use this command to hot-plug the raw block iSCSI LUN into an active VM. It passes through as a generic SCSI block device.

Command  | virsh attach-disk --domain database-vm \
         |                    --source /dev/disk/by-path/ip-192.168.100.20:3260-iscsi-://2026-08.com.example...lun-1 \
         |                    --target vdc \
         |                    --cache none \
         |                    --live \
         |                    --config
         |
Fields   | --domain  : The exact name of your targeted virtual machine (e.g., database-vm).
         | --source  : The absolute device path retrieved from Phase 2, Step 1.
         | --target  : Virtual disk device label exposed to the VM (vdc, vdd, etc.).
         | --cache   : Set to "none" for shared/network clustering block layers to maintain consistency.
         | --live    : Hot-plugs the storage layout immediately into the active operational workspace.
         | --config  : Writes the change permanently to the VM's primary core configuration repository.

